From 6461b3be880698c0020d2fe24730367e4da30162 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Geramy <geramy@local>
Date: Tue, 12 May 2026 21:28:40 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] amdgpu: small-BAR fallback for Apple Silicon TB5 passthrough

When the host has a tiny prefetchable memory window (e.g. ~260MB on each
Apple Silicon TB5 downstream port), rebar cannot grow BAR0 to match the
VRAM size. The existing code releases BAR0/BAR2, then pci_resize_resource
fails, leaving them IORESOURCE_UNSET, and amdgpu_device_resize_fb_bar
returns -ENODEV -- which fails sw_init of gmc_v12_0.

Detect this case up front (no rebar size advertised beyond current) and
keep the current BAR assignment, clamping visible_vram_size to BAR len so
TTM only CPU-maps what is reachable. This is the same strategy
tinygrad/AM uses to bring up RDNA3/RDNA4 cards over USB4/TB5 on macOS.

Co-developed-with: tinygrad/AM observations
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_device.c | 37 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 37 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_device.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_device.c
index 6d8531f..b202781 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_device.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_device.c
@@ -1844,6 +1844,43 @@ int amdgpu_device_resize_fb_bar(struct amdgpu_device *adev)
 	    (pci_resource_len(adev->pdev, 0) >= adev->gmc.real_vram_size))
 		return 0;
 
+	/*
+	 * Small-BAR fallback (Apple Silicon Thunderbolt 5 passthrough).
+	 *
+	 * Compare the rebar capability's MAX advertised size (in bytes) to the
+	 * current BAR0 length. If we can't actually grow, short-circuit:
+	 * releasing BAR0/BAR2 below without a successful resize leaves them
+	 * IORESOURCE_UNSET and the function returns -ENODEV.
+	 *
+	 * NOTE: we deliberately do NOT use pci_rebar_get_possible_sizes() bitmap
+	 * for the check. QEMU's vfio_setup_rebar_ecap rewrites the rebar
+	 * Capability Register to advertise sizes based on the rebar Control
+	 * Register's size field, which can disagree with pci_resource_len(0)
+	 * (the BAR was programmed to a different size than what rebar ctrl
+	 * reports). A direct bytes comparison is the source of truth.
+	 *
+	 * Clamp visible_vram_size to BAR len so TTM only CPU-maps what we can
+	 * reach; non-visible VRAM is reached via SDMA, the same approach
+	 * tinygrad's userspace AM driver uses on this exact hardware.
+	 */
+	{
+		int max_size = pci_rebar_get_max_size(adev->pdev, 0);
+		u64 bar_len = pci_resource_len(adev->pdev, 0);
+		u64 max_bytes = (max_size >= 0) ? (1ULL << (max_size + 20)) : 0;
+
+		if (max_bytes <= bar_len) {
+			dev_info(adev->dev,
+				 "small-BAR mode: BAR0=%lluM, VRAM=%lluM, "
+				 "rebar max=%lluM (cannot grow) -- skipping resize\n",
+				 bar_len >> 20,
+				 adev->gmc.real_vram_size >> 20,
+				 max_bytes >> 20);
+			if (adev->gmc.visible_vram_size > bar_len)
+				adev->gmc.visible_vram_size = bar_len;
+			return 0;
+		}
+	}
+
 	/* Check if the root BUS has 64bit memory resources */
 	root = adev->pdev->bus;
 	while (root->parent)
-- 
2.53.0

